  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper low currently rotating over southern Saskatchewan will\nmove through northern ND tonight, then open up and dig into\nnorthern MN tomorrow. Another reinforcing shortwave come down\ninto ONT and then off into the Great Lakes Sunday night and\nMonday, with surface high pressure building in for early next\nweek. Another upper low swings through the region quickly late \nTuesday and into Wednesday, but not much surface reflection and\nensemble precip probabilities are 20 percent or less. A bit of \na break for the later part of next week, then another shortwave\non Saturday. \n\n...Light snow accumulations in north tonight and tomorrow...\n\nMost of the larger scale models as well as the CAMs have the\nbest QPF swinging through our northern counties during the day\ntomorrow. Probabilities of at least a tenth of an inch of snow\nare above 60 percent north of Highway 2, and some areas in\nnortheastern ND have up to 50 percent chance for an inch. Think\nthe better chances for an inch or more of accumulation will be\ndownwind of Lake of the Woods, but wind direction not favorable\nfor lake effect in our CWA. Ground temperatures still fairly\nwarm, but METRo has readings dipping well below the freezing\nmark overnight and staying there most of tomorrow morning. Under\nthe current position of the upper low in southwestern\nSaskatchewan, roads are partially covered with some and web cams\ndo show some spots with light accumulations. Expecting the low\nto weaken a bit as it moves southeast, but could still see some\ndecent snow rates and light accumulation. Given this is the \nfirst event with any kind of winter impact for the season, will \nput out a Special Weather Statement even with accumulations not \nexpected to reach advisory criteria.\n\n\n...More seasonable temperatures next week...\n\nAlong with the upper low tomorrow will come a reinforcing shot\nof cold air, with temperatures from 850mb downward to the\nsurface below the freezing mark. Could even see some single\ndigits Sunday night depending on cloud cover under the surface\nhigh. The cold early in the week will just be slightly on the \ncooler side of normal though, and then steady back out with a \nslight warming trend as we head through the rest of next week.",
